#!/usr/bin/bash
# =============================================================================
# aquarius-session-lib — the tidying-up half of the Aquarius Desktop
# =============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS NOT A PROGRAM. It is a list of names and a handful of functions
# that two other files read:
#
#   /usr/bin/aquarius-session          starts the desktop, and calls the
#                                      clean-up in here when it ends
#   /usr/libexec/aquarius-session-portals
#                                      the small helper the window manager runs
#                                      at login to make the portals ours
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL — THE BENCH BUG, 2026-09-04
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Royce reported it in one sentence: "when logging out of Aquarius to go to
# GNOME, it kicks me back to the login screen a couple of times before letting
# me log in."
#
# Here is what was actually happening, in plain language.
#
# When you log in, Linux starts a personal background manager for you — systemd
# calls it the "user manager". It outlives any one desktop. Think of it as a
# noticeboard plus a set of running helpers that belong to YOU rather than to
# one login.
#
# The Aquarius Desktop pins things to that noticeboard on purpose. It writes
# down "the screen is called wayland-0", "this desktop is called Aquarius",
# "our settings live in /usr/share/aquarius/shell", and a few more. Anything
# started later — a file dialog, a screen recorder, a password prompt — reads
# the noticeboard so it knows where it is. That is not a hack, it is how every
# Wayland desktop works, GNOME included.
#
# THE BUG WAS THAT NOBODY EVER RUBBED THE NOTICEBOARD OUT AGAIN.
#
# So you logged out of Aquarius, and left behind:
#
#   * notes saying the screen is wayland-0 — a screen that no longer exists,
#   * a note saying this desktop is called "Aquarius", which is the note the
#     portal system reads to decide that screen recording should go to the
#     wlroots back end — a back end that only works with OUR window manager,
#   * helpers still running and still believing all of the above: the bar
#     (which owns the name every notification in the system is delivered to),
#     the wallpaper program, the keyboard remapper, the wlroots portal.
#
# Then GNOME started, and tried to be the desktop. It could not take the
# notification name, because our bar still had it. Its portals were already
# running, pointed at a compositor that had died. Its own session pieces waited
# for services that were never going to answer, timed out, gave up — and GDM
# does exactly one thing when a session gives up during start-up: it puts the
# login screen back. Try again, same leftovers, same failure. Only after the
# stragglers had been killed off by their own timeouts — a minute or so, i.e.
# "a couple of times" — was the machine clean enough for GNOME to start.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE WE WERE BREAKING — IT IS WRITTEN DOWN, AND WE WERE NOT FOLLOWING IT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `man systemd.special`, under graphical-session.target, says a session manager
# must do TWO things when the session ends:
#
#   1. stop graphical-session.target, so everything that said "start me with the
#      graphical session" is stopped with it, and
#   2. UNSET the variables it set, so the next session does not inherit them.
#
# We did the first (in the window manager's `shutdown` file) and never did the
# second. GNOME does both: gnome-session imports its environment into the user
# manager when it starts and removes it again when it stops. That difference is
# the whole bug.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE LISTS LIVE HERE AND NOT IN THE LAUNCHER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is one obvious way for this fix to rot: somebody adds a new variable to
# the launcher, forgets to add it to the clean-up list, and six months later
# there is a mystery that looks nothing like a missing line in a list.
#
# So the SAME LIST is used for both jobs. The launcher does not name the
# variables it hands to systemd — it hands over AQ_SESSION_ENV_PUSH, from this
# file. The clean-up removes AQ_SESSION_ENV_UNSET, computed from that same
# list. And tests/test-session-teardown.sh reads the launcher, finds every
# `export` in it, and fails the build if any of them is missing from the list
# here. Drift is not a matter of discipline; it is a build failure.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE VARIABLES THE LAUNCHER PUTS ON THE NOTICEBOARD
#
# This is the list /usr/bin/aquarius-session hands to systemd and D-Bus. Every
# `export` in that file must appear here — the test enforces it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_ENV_PUSH="XDG_SESSION_TYPE XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP QS_CONFIG_PATH QT_QPA_PLATFORM AQ_LOG AQ_UI_SCALE LANG"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO THE WINDOW MANAGER ADDS LATER
#
# These cannot be in the list above: they do not exist when the launcher runs,
# because the window manager has not started and there is no screen yet. The
# labwc `autostart` file adds them a moment later. They still have to be taken
# down at the end, and WAYLAND_DISPLAY is the single most poisonous one to
# leave behind — it names a socket that is gone.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_ENV_COMPOSITOR="WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE WE DELIBERATELY LEAVE ALONE
#
# LANG is not a fact about this desktop. It is a fact about this PERSON: which
# language they read, and which alphabet their filenames are written in. It is
# the same answer in GNOME as it is here.
#
# Removing it would also walk straight back into the bug of 2026-09-03. When
# nothing sets LANG, programs fall back to the "C" locale, whose alphabet is
# 1968-era 7-bit ASCII, and accented characters start being drawn as boxes. The
# launcher goes to real trouble to make sure LANG is right (see the long
# comment in it). Un-setting that on the way out would be undoing our own work.
#
# So: pushed, never unset. The test checks both halves of that sentence.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_ENV_KEEP="LANG"

# The list the clean-up actually removes: everything we set, minus the keeper.
# Computed rather than typed, so the three lists above can never disagree.
aq_session_env_unset_list() {
    local aq_name aq_keep aq_skip aq_out=""
    for aq_name in ${AQ_SESSION_ENV_PUSH} ${AQ_SESSION_ENV_COMPOSITOR}; do
        aq_skip=0
        for aq_keep in ${AQ_SESSION_ENV_KEEP}; do
            if [ "${aq_name}" = "${aq_keep}" ]; then
                aq_skip=1
            fi
        done
        if [ "${aq_skip}" -eq 0 ]; then
            aq_out="${aq_out}${aq_out:+ }${aq_name}"
        fi
    done
    printf '%s\n' "${aq_out}"
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PORTALS
#
# A "portal" is the middleman a sandboxed application talks to when it wants to
# record the screen or open a file. Which back end answers is decided by the
# XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP note on the noticeboard, ONCE, when the portal starts.
#
# That "once" is the problem in both directions:
#
#   * A portal started under Aquarius has read "Aquarius" and sends screen
#     recording to the wlroots back end. If it survives into a GNOME session it
#     is still doing that, against a compositor that no longer exists.
#   * A portal started under GNOME has read "GNOME" and ignores our
#     aquarius-portals.conf entirely. If it survives into an Aquarius session,
#     OBS shows an empty list of screens and nothing anywhere says why.
#
# The fix for both is the same and it is safe: STOP them. Every one of these is
# started on demand — the moment an application asks for a portal, D-Bus starts
# one again, and the new one reads the noticeboard as it is now. Stopping a
# portal is never destructive; it is closing a shop that reopens when somebody
# knocks.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_PORTAL_UNITS="xdg-desktop-portal.service xdg-desktop-portal-wlr.service xdg-desktop-portal-gtk.service xdg-desktop-portal-gnome.service"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERYTHING TO STOP, IN THE ORDER IT IS STOPPED
#
# Leaves first, then the trunk. The two targets at the end are the ones
# `man systemd.special` names: labwc-session.target is bound to
# graphical-session.target, and anything that said "start me with the graphical
# session" — our keyboard remapper, the keyring, the portals — is PartOf it and
# goes down with it.
#
# The portals and the remapper are named individually anyway, ahead of the
# targets, and that redundancy is on purpose. `PartOf=` only stops a unit if the
# unit really carries that line; naming them here means the clean-up does not
# depend on a stranger's unit file being written the way we hope.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_UNITS="aquarius-keys.service ${AQ_SESSION_PORTAL_UNITS} labwc-session.target graphical-session.target"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROGRAMS THAT DO NOT BELONG TO SYSTEMD, AND SO HAVE TO BE ASKED DIRECTLY
#
# ⚠️ Linux does NOT kill your programs when you log out. Fedora ships
# systemd-logind with KillUserProcesses=no, which means a program the window
# manager started keeps running after the window manager is gone. That is a
# deliberate upstream choice (it is what lets a long build survive a logout) and
# we are not going to change it machine-wide for one desktop.
#
# But it means these four are ours to clean up, because nothing else will:
#
#   qs / quickshell   the Aquarius bar. THE IMPORTANT ONE. It owns
#                     org.freedesktop.Notifications — the single name every
#                     notification on the machine is delivered to. GNOME's shell
#                     wants that name too, and there is only one. A surviving
#                     bar is a GNOME session with no notifications.
#                     ("qs" and "quickshell" are the same program under two
#                     names; which one shows up depends on how it was started,
#                     so both are listed.)
#   swaybg            the wallpaper. Harmless, but it is holding the old screen
#                     open and it is confusing to find running the next day.
#   xremap-wlroots    the keyboard remapper. It has a grab on the keyboards. A
#   xremap-gnome      leftover copy is exactly what broke the remapper on
#                     2026-09-03 — the new one asked for the keyboards and was
#                     told "device or resource busy".
#   slurp             the little "which screen?" overlay. Only running if a
#                     recording was being set up as the session ended.
#   xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
#                     the screen-recording back end. Stopping its unit above is
#                     the proper way; this is the belt to that braces, because
#                     it is the one portal that is genuinely useless without our
#                     compositor.
#
# NOT in this list, on purpose: xdg-desktop-portal and xdg-desktop-portal-gtk.
# Those are shared with every other desktop, they are started on demand, and
# stopping their unit is the right lever. Reaching past systemd to kill them by
# name could reach into a second session this person has open on another screen.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AQ_SESSION_STRAGGLERS="qs quickshell swaybg xremap-wlroots xremap-gnome slurp xdg-desktop-portal-wlr"

# How long we are willing to wait for the graphical session to actually stop,
# in seconds. Long enough for a slow machine, short enough that a stuck service
# cannot turn a logout into a hang. Anything still alive after this is killed by
# name below, which is the whole point of having both.
AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT="${AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT:-10}"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Saying what we are doing.
#
# Straight into the same session log as everything else, in the same plain
# language, because the person reading it is not a systems programmer — and
# because "did the clean-up run?" is the first question anybody will ask the
# next time a login misbehaves.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_say() {
    echo "[logout] $*"
}

# Run something, but never let it hang the logout. `timeout` is part of
# coreutils and is always there; the fallback is only so that this file can be
# read and tested anywhere.
aq_session_bounded() {
    local aq_secs="$1"
    shift
    if command -v timeout > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        timeout "${aq_secs}" "$@" > /dev/null 2>&1
    else
        "$@" > /dev/null 2>&1
    fi
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1 — stop the services.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_stop_units() {
    aq_session_say "stopping the desktop's background services"
    # One `stop` for the whole list: systemd works out the order itself, and a
    # unit that is not installed or was never started is not an error worth
    # printing at somebody who is trying to log out.
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    aq_session_bounded "${AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT}" \
        systemctl --user stop ${AQ_SESSION_UNITS} || true
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2 — wait, but not forever, for the graphical session to really be over.
#
# `systemctl stop` normally waits by itself. Normally. A service that ignores
# the polite request to stop gets ninety seconds before systemd loses patience,
# and ninety seconds of black screen is exactly the experience this whole file
# exists to prevent. So we ask, we watch, and after AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT we
# stop watching and move on to the blunter tools.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_wait_for_stop() {
    local aq_left="${AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT}"
    while [ "${aq_left}" -gt 0 ]; do
        if ! systemctl --user is-active graphical-session.target > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            aq_session_say "the graphical session has stopped"
            return 0
        fi
        sleep 1
        aq_left=$((aq_left - 1))
    done
    aq_session_say "the graphical session did not stop within ${AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT} seconds; carrying on anyway"
    return 1
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 3 — rub the noticeboard out. THIS IS THE ACTUAL FIX.
#
# Two noticeboards, and they take different instructions:
#
#   systemd's       has a real "forget this" — `unset-environment`. Used.
#                   This is the one that matters on Fedora, because D-Bus here
#                   asks systemd to start services, so systemd's copy of the
#                   environment is the copy those services are handed.
#
#   D-Bus's own     has NO "forget this". `dbus-update-activation-environment`
#                   can only set values. The nearest thing to erasing a note is
#                   to write an empty one, which is what GNOME's session does
#                   too, and it is enough: an empty XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP makes
#                   the portal fall back to its default configuration instead of
#                   confidently loading OURS in the middle of a GNOME session.
#
# ⚠️ The D-Bus pass deliberately does NOT use --systemd. With that flag it would
# push the empty values into systemd as well, leaving XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP set to
# "" there instead of genuinely gone — undoing the good half of this function.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_unset_env() {
    local aq_names aq_name aq_blanks=""
    aq_names="$(aq_session_env_unset_list)"

    aq_session_say "forgetting this desktop's settings: ${aq_names}"

    if command -v dbus-update-activation-environment > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        for aq_name in ${aq_names}; do
            aq_blanks="${aq_blanks}${aq_blanks:+ }${aq_name}="
        done
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        dbus-update-activation-environment ${aq_blanks} > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
    fi

    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    systemctl --user unset-environment ${aq_names} > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 4 — the programs systemd does not know about.
#
# Politely first (a normal stop request), then firmly. Every name comes from the
# fixed list at the top of this file, every kill is limited to this person's own
# programs with --uid, and --exact means the name must match completely — so
# "qs" cannot possibly match somebody's "qsomething".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_kill_stragglers() {
    local aq_name aq_found=""

    if ! command -v pkill > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        aq_session_say "pkill is not installed, so leftover programs cannot be tidied up"
        return 0
    fi

    for aq_name in ${AQ_SESSION_STRAGGLERS}; do
        if pkill --uid "$(id -u)" --exact --signal TERM "${aq_name}" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            aq_found="${aq_found}${aq_found:+ }${aq_name}"
        fi
    done

    if [ -z "${aq_found}" ]; then
        aq_session_say "nothing was left running"
        return 0
    fi

    aq_session_say "asked these to close: ${aq_found}"

    # Three seconds is a long time for a program that has been asked to close
    # and has nothing to save. Anything still there after it is not going to.
    sleep 3

    for aq_name in ${aq_found}; do
        if pkill --uid "$(id -u)" --exact --signal KILL "${aq_name}" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            aq_session_say "${aq_name} would not close and was stopped"
        fi
    done
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 5 — clear the failure marks.
#
# A service that was stopped mid-job can be left marked "failed". systemd will
# refuse to start a unit that has failed too many times too quickly, so the next
# login could inherit a keyboard remapper that will not start and no explanation
# for it. One command, and the slate is clean.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_reset_failed() {
    systemctl --user reset-failed > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole thing, in order. Called from /usr/bin/aquarius-session on EVERY way
# out of the session — the normal Super+Shift+E, a crash of the window manager,
# or the launcher failing before it ever got started.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_teardown() {
    # Not a single step in here is allowed to abort the logout, so the launcher's
    # "stop at the first error" rule is switched off for the duration. We are on
    # the way out; there is nothing left to protect.
    set +e

    aq_session_say "cleaning up so the next login starts fresh"
    aq_session_stop_units
    aq_session_wait_for_stop
    aq_session_unset_env
    aq_session_kill_stragglers
    aq_session_reset_failed
    aq_session_say "done — GNOME or Aquarius will start from a clean slate"
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AND THE SAME PROBLEM FROM THE OTHER SIDE: starting up.
#
# GNOME has exactly the same habit we just fixed in ourselves — it leaves its
# portals running. So a person who logs out of GNOME and into Aquarius arrives
# to find xdg-desktop-portal already awake, already told it is in GNOME, and
# completely uninterested in aquarius-portals.conf. Symptom: OBS lists no
# screens to record, and nothing anywhere says why.
#
# So we stop them at the START of our session too. They come straight back the
# first time anything asks — and the one that comes back reads OUR settings.
#
# This runs from the window manager's autostart file, not from the launcher,
# because it has to happen AFTER WAYLAND_DISPLAY exists. A portal restarted
# before there is a screen would only have to be restarted again.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_session_portals_refresh() {
    aq_session_say "restarting the portals so they pick up the Aquarius settings"
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    aq_session_bounded "${AQ_SESSION_STOP_TIMEOUT}" \
        systemctl --user stop ${AQ_SESSION_PORTAL_UNITS} || true
}
